// =============================================================================
// yolo_conv_engine.sv — tiled INT8 convolution over the systolic array
//
// The array is ARRAY_SIZE x ARRAY_SIZE. Real YOLO26n layers are up to
// 256 in-channels x 256 out-channels over a 160x160 map, so every layer is a
// three-level tile loop:
//
//   for oc_tile in ceil(out_c / ARRAY_SIZE):        <- weight tile columns
//     for ic_tile in ceil(k*k*in_c / ARRAY_SIZE):   <- reduction depth
//       load weight tile (ARRAY_SIZE x ARRAY_SIZE INT8)
//       for each output position (out_w * out_h):
//         stream the lowered activation column, MAC into the accumulator
//     requantize the ARRAY_SIZE accumulators -> INT8, write out
//
// Accumulators live in the output BRAM at INT32 across the whole ic_tile loop
// and are only requantized on the LAST ic_tile. Requantizing per tile would
// clamp mid-reduction and silently change the numerics -- see the note in
// yolo_layer_sequencer.sv.
//
// Weight layout the exporter must produce (GEMM layout, not PyTorch layout):
//   for each oc_tile, for each ic_tile: an ARRAY_SIZE x ARRAY_SIZE INT8 tile,
//   row = reduction index (k*k*in_c), column = output channel, row-major,
//   zero padded when the layer does not fill the tile.
// Emitting these in the loop order above means the engine reads weights
// strictly sequentially and never seeks -- worth the exporter complexity.
//
// Depthwise convs (LAYER_F_DEPTHWISE) reduce over k*k only, with out_c == in_c
// and one group per channel. The exporter still emits square tiles; the engine
// just uses a different reduction extent.
// =============================================================================
`timescale 1ns/1ps

